WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.350
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book one.

2
00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:09.118
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.418 --> 00:00:15.460
Unit thirteen: Thesis Development. Today you will refine claims using comparative structures.

4
00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:18.227
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.167 --> 00:00:22.678
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.517 --> 00:00:24.889
Thesis statement.

7
00:00:25.883 --> 00:00:31.273
Thesis statement means a single sentence expressing the central, arguable claim of a text.

8
00:00:31.883 --> 00:00:34.881
A thesis statement should be debatable, not a fact.

9
00:00:35.483 --> 00:00:38.112
Your turn. Say: Thesis statement.

10
00:00:40.967 --> 00:00:42.246
Thesis statement.

11
00:00:42.983 --> 00:00:43.926
Argument.

12
00:00:44.917 --> 00:00:48.507
Argument means a connected series of claims supported by evidence.

13
00:00:49.117 --> 00:00:52.219
Each body paragraph advances the argument one step.

14
00:00:52.817 --> 00:00:55.202
Your turn. Say: Argument.

15
00:00:58.067 --> 00:00:59.010
Argument.

16
00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:00.612
Scope.

17
00:01:01.617 --> 00:01:04.650
Scope means the extent of the area a study deals with.

18
00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:07.993
Limiting the scope made the project manageable.

19
00:01:08.583 --> 00:01:11.282
Your turn. Say: Scope.

20
00:01:14.133 --> 00:01:14.995
Scope.

21
00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:16.414
Claim.

22
00:01:17.417 --> 00:01:19.788
Claim means an assertion that something is true.

23
00:01:20.383 --> 00:01:23.068
An unsupported claim weakens the whole essay.

24
00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:25.802
Your turn. Say: Claim.

25
00:01:28.667 --> 00:01:29.447
Claim.

26
00:01:30.183 --> 00:01:31.486
Counterargument.

27
00:01:32.483 --> 00:01:36.213
Counterargument means an opposing view that the writer must address.

28
00:01:36.817 --> 00:01:40.198
Addressing the counterargument shows intellectual honesty.

29
00:01:40.783 --> 00:01:43.285
Your turn. Say: Counterargument.

30
00:01:46.133 --> 00:01:47.204
Counterargument.

31
00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:48.812
Refinement.

32
00:01:49.817 --> 00:01:54.278
Refinement means an improvement achieved through small, precise changes.

33
00:01:54.883 --> 00:01:57.231
Several refinements sharpened the thesis.

34
00:01:57.817 --> 00:02:00.237
Your turn. Say: Refinement.

35
00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:04.020
Refinement.

36
00:02:04.783 --> 00:02:05.854
Focus.

37
00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:09.581
Focus means the central point of concentration.

38
00:02:10.183 --> 00:02:12.589
The essay lost focus in the second half.

39
00:02:13.183 --> 00:02:16.230
Your turn. Say: Focus.

40
00:02:19.067 --> 00:02:20.161
Focus.

41
00:02:20.917 --> 00:02:22.104
Originality.

42
00:02:23.117 --> 00:02:27.043
Originality means the quality of offering a new contribution.

43
00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:31.356
Originality does not require a revolutionary idea.

44
00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:34.811
Your turn. Say: Originality.

45
00:02:37.667 --> 00:02:38.888
Originality.

46
00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:43.270
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:44.117 --> 00:02:49.056
Visal, your draft thesis is 'Social media affects teenagers.' What do you think of it now?

48
00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:57.586
It's far too broad. A narrower thesis is often stronger than a broad one, and that statement is barely arguable.

49
00:02:58.683 --> 00:03:00.160
How have you refined it?

50
00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:11.530
'Image-based platforms harm teenage girls' self-esteem more severely than text-based ones.' It's more specific and considerably more debatable.

51
00:03:12.617 --> 00:03:15.197
Isn't a narrower claim less important, though?

52
00:03:16.283 --> 00:03:22.279
Not necessarily. The more precise the claim, the more convincingly I can support it.

53
00:03:23.433 --> 00:03:25.154
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:26.017 --> 00:03:28.655
Grammar focus: Comparative structures.

55
00:03:28.955 --> 00:03:30.049
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:33.957
A narrower thesis is often stronger than a broad one.

57
00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:39.381
Image-based platforms harm self-esteem more severely than text-based ones.

58
00:03:40.283 --> 00:03:43.931
The more precise the claim, the more convincingly I can support it.

59
00:03:44.867 --> 00:03:53.881
Modify comparatives with far, considerably or slightly for precision. Use 'the + comparative …, the + comparative …' to show that two things change together.

60
00:03:54.617 --> 00:03:58.604
Be careful! Don't say: A narrower thesis is more stronger.

61
00:04:00.567 --> 00:04:03.263
Say: A narrower thesis is stronger.

62
00:04:03.563 --> 00:04:05.620
Never combine 'more' with an -er form.

63
00:04:06.433 --> 00:04:08.946
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:09.733 --> 00:04:11.373
From Topic to Thesis.

65
00:04:11.673 --> 00:04:16.517
Many students confuse a topic with a thesis, yet the two are fundamentally different.

66
00:04:17.533 --> 00:04:23.051
A topic names an area of interest, whereas a thesis makes a specific, arguable claim about it.

67
00:04:24.067 --> 00:04:29.491
A narrower thesis is often stronger than a broad one, because it can be supported in greater depth.

68
00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:35.971
The more directly a writer anticipates counterarguments, the more persuasive the final essay becomes.

69
00:04:36.967 --> 00:04:42.681
Through successive refinements, a vague idea gradually becomes a focused and original contribution.

70
00:04:43.767 --> 00:04:47.699
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:51.861
A narrower thesis is often stronger blank a broad one.

72
00:04:52.161 --> 00:04:54.886
A, that. B, then. C, than.

73
00:04:58.433 --> 00:05:00.015
The answer is C: than.

74
00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:03.695
The more precise the claim, blank I can support it.

75
00:05:03.995 --> 00:05:09.297
A, the more convincingly. B, more convincing. C, the most convincingly.

76
00:05:12.833 --> 00:05:15.611
The answer is A: the more convincingly.

77
00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:18.831
Which is the strongest thesis statement?

78
00:05:19.131 --> 00:05:28.903
A, Social media affects teenagers.. B, Image-based apps harm girls' self-esteem most.. C, Social media is a topic many people discuss..

79
00:05:32.467 --> 00:05:36.533
The answer is B: Image-based apps harm girls' self-esteem most..

80
00:05:37.683 --> 00:05:40.879
Which modifier correctly intensifies a comparative?

81
00:05:41.179 --> 00:05:46.667
A, very stronger. B, considerably stronger. C, too much stronger.

82
00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:53.128
The answer is B: considerably stronger.

83
00:05:54.283 --> 00:05:58.721
According to the reading, what is the difference between a topic and a thesis?

84
00:05:59.021 --> 00:06:06.076
A, A thesis makes an arguable claim.. B, A topic is always longer.. C, There is no difference..

85
00:06:09.633 --> 00:06:13.084
The answer is A: A thesis makes an arguable claim..

86
00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:17.301
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:21.090
What makes a thesis statement strong rather than weak?

88
00:06:37.883 --> 00:06:39.302
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:39.602 --> 00:07:15.038
A strong thesis statement is far more specific than a weak one, and it makes a claim that a reasonable person could dispute. For example, 'online learning is good' is vague, whereas 'online learning benefits self-motivated students more than others' gives the essay a clear direction. A strong thesis also limits its scope realistically. Some writers fear that a narrow claim sounds less important. In fact, the more precise the thesis, the more convincingly it can be defended.

90
00:07:15.338 --> 00:07:17.082
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:17.983 --> 00:07:22.424
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and comparative structures.

92
00:07:22.724 --> 00:07:26.630
Today's value is precision. Choose your words and numbers with care.

93
00:07:26.930 --> 00:07:29.814
See you in Unit fourteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:30.114 --> 00:07:32.581
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

